RjY wrote:

> Sorry for the stupid question but what's a software overlay?
> Google is just giving me irrelevancies about video playback.

It's a technique for writing programs to run in a very small 
memory space, now used mainly on "bittyboxes" so constrained 
that they can't even afford an OS-based or hardware-based 
virtual memory manager.  In fact, now used mainly on boxes so 
constrained as not to have an OS at all, in programs intended 
to run on bare metal.  The last time it was a widely known 
part of computer programming that any pro had better know how 
to handle was when DOS and CP/M were considered professional-
level operating systems. 

In overlay code, you twiddle directly with the linker (if you 
even have a linker) to find out the absolute address at which 
some routine (the "overlay routine") is loaded.  You have a 
different routine (the "overlay manager") that reads a block 
(an "overlay block") from the disk and loads it directly into 
the memory space occupied by the overlay routine, overwriting 
the machine code there.  The overlay manager remembers which 
block is loaded so that a request for the same block won't 
result in a useless disk read/block load delay. 

Now your overlay routine does a different thing depending on which 
overlay block has been most recently loaded.  So if you have, say, 
fifteen or twenty different routines you can't afford to keep in 
memory, you make overlay blocks containing machine instructions to 
carry out the logic of those routines.  Any time you need one of 
them to run, you call your overlay manager to load it, then call
the overlay routine.  

People being clever would often use the program's initializer 
for the overlay routine; it becomes dead code after the first 
time it runs, so it saves you one block that you would otherwise
need a way to reload.  

There were a lot of variations on that general scheme; Sometimes 
an overlay block would have multiple entry points, for example. 
Some programs used multiple overlay routines.  Some compilers 
were smart enough to eliminate redundant calls to the overlay 
manager.  Usually all the different overlay blocks had to contain 
routines with the same argument signature (although "type punning" 
was used a lot; I still remember working out which sequence of ten 
integers had the same bit pattern in memory as a short float 
followed by a particular null-terminated string, for example). 


